WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.971
- All right, we just gotta,

00:00:00.971 --> 00:00:02.250
we just gotta talk about
something real quick.

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:05.370
So, one of the biggest
announcements at Google's fall event

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.690
that they just had was
that they're planning

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.120
on supporting the Pixel 8 and Pixel 8 Pro

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.120
with seven years of OS security
and feature drop updates.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.570
This is industry leading
and super awesome.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:19.890
Here's a list of some
of the other promises

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.180
that have been made in
the smartphone world

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.920
for how long down the road they plan

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.274
on supporting their devices.

00:00:24.274 --> 00:00:26.700
In the world of Android,
this is unprecedented.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.631
So, clearly this was one
of the best announcements

00:00:28.631 --> 00:00:30.270
of the entire Google event,

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.820
and we are finally happy to see that.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.820
But on the other hand,
Google has a rich history

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.150
of discontinuing products
and abandoning projects

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.070
and services that have a
lot of people using them

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:48.210
or depending on them, so how
do we square those two things?

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.490
Like you would think that
relying on a big company

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.350
is better than relying on a small one,

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.240
as far as like supporting
projects down the road.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.030
Like if I was gonna set up a
new smartphone, for example,

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.190
I wouldn't wanna set up one
with like a tiny startup

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.050
that I don't know if they're
gonna exist in five years

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.050
'cause then all my smartphone
stuff might not work

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.250
when that company goes outta business.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.590
So, you'd think a bigger,
more established company

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.780
would be more reliable, but it turns out,

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.150
the same problem can
happen when you rely on

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.700
a gigantic company like Google

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.380
because something you
might get invested in

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.410
or start using might just be
like a small rounding error

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.380
for them that they might
just get rid of at any time.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.510
So, I think the thing
that really summarizes

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.890
this all well is I have a friend that says

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.830
that Google is super willing
to spin up new projects

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.938
and be aggressive and
innovative all the time,

00:01:30.938 --> 00:01:34.320
but if that new project doesn't
either get a billion users

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:35.880
or make a billion dollars,

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.360
then it is liable to get axed at any time.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.610
So, that's why literally
just in the past few weeks,

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:44.700
we have seen Google
Domains, Google Jamboard,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.120
Google Podcasts, and
Pixel Pass all killed,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.430
all active services with various amounts

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.950
of users just destroyed, just killed.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.020
And the further back you
go, the more there are.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.490
Like this, it feels like a running joke

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.510
that Google will announce

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.337
and kill a new messaging
service every year,

00:01:59.337 --> 00:02:01.500
but just keep scrolling back.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.080
Like there's entire webpages
dedicated to chronicling

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.020
the long list of things
that Google has killed.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.780
Remember Google Plus,
remember the Inbox app.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.850
Remember Hangouts, remember Aleo.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:15.030
Remember Chromecast Audio,
Stadia, the list goes on.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.220
Now, when they kill these
products or services,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.160
there are often various
degrees to which they're able

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.440
to replace them or support everyone

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.270
that they just rug pulled.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.000
So, like something like
Google Play Music, okay,

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.340
they were launching another
service called YouTube Music

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.780
and it had a lot of congruent features,

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.150
so they didn't have the need to have both.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.280
So, they made it easy to
port your entire library

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.600
from one to the other.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.010
You'd still be missing some features,

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:39.720
but at least you still
have your whole library.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:40.553
So, that was nice.

00:02:40.553 --> 00:02:42.060
There's a similar thing
with Google Podcasts.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.630
Like it was a pretty solid
podcast app, but they again,

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.720
wanted to bring those
things into YouTube Music.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.520
So, they've worked on an import process

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:51.990
and some tutorials for that.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.060
And like it's a good effort.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.160
It's good to at least try
to do that sort of thing,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.300
but it's not always perfect.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.590
Google Jamboard, you might not have heard

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.351
of Google Jamboard, but weirdly enough,

00:03:00.351 --> 00:03:02.400
the old pro Ultimate
Frisbee team I've played on,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.350
New York Empire, uses Google Jamboard

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.940
for like our scouting documents,

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:07.470
for like writing everything down

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.200
and players and tendencies
and all that sort of thing.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.700
And it's gone now, or at least by the end

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:12.533
of the year it'll be gone.

00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:16.050
So, I guess we'll just
find something else to use.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:17.858
Google Stadia, the gaming
service only lasted

00:03:17.858 --> 00:03:19.470
a couple of years.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.709
The only saving grace with
this one is when it got axed,

00:03:21.709 --> 00:03:24.180
they would at least let
you export your saved data,

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.370
so you could use it on another platform.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.730
And then, they refunded every
Stadia user for every game.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.100
But again, there are no alternatives.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.780
Fun fact, and this is a true story.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.200
I had literally just switched mkbhd.com

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.900
from Squarespace to Google Domains.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.000
It was like a big domain transfer process

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.490
that took like 24 hours and
I was like nervous to do it,

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.430
but I finally did it
and then within a week,

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.500
Google killed Google Domains

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.120
and sold all of those
domains back to Squarespace.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.580
So, there are no Google Domains anymore,

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.460
but maybe the craziest recent
one would be Pixel Pass.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:00.420
Pixel Pass is crazy.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:01.770
So, if you haven't heard of it,

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.630
it was basically a subscription
plan that was launched

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.840
alongside the Pixel 6, and
the idea was you sign up

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.060
and then you pay 45 bucks a month,

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.170
you get the brand new phone,

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.960
and then you get YouTube
premium, YouTube music,

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.630
a bunch of Google 1 storage,
and some other stuff,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.220
all included packaged together.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.790
And then, you get a new Pixel
for free every two years,

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:22.860
which I mean, sounds pretty sweet, right?

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.090
If you use a bunch of Google services,

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.140
they're all packaged together,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.432
the overall price would
be a little bit lower

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:29.070
than if you'd bought all of
these things individually.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.487
And then, who wouldn't want to upgrade

00:04:30.487 --> 00:04:33.570
to a new Pixel phone roughly
once every two years?

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:34.590
Sounds pretty sweet.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:36.600
A bunch of people signed up,

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:41.600
and then, exactly 22 months
after they announced it,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.670
so a month and a half before
the first upgrade cycle,

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.680
they quietly killed the program

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.710
and nobody got their free phone upgrade

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.930
because they killed it right
before they were supposed to.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.850
And there have been no
announced alternatives.

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:55.110
And the list goes on and on and on,

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:59.040
but clearly this type of
behavior erodes your trust

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.420
in anything Google.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.390
Me, personally, I was thinking about maybe

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:04.710
switching to Google Fi.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.223
There's a bunch of other
carriers out there.

00:05:06.223 --> 00:05:07.950
I was gonna maybe try it.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:09.810
The integration with the Pixel seems cool,

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.720
but I cannot be sure
that it'll still exist

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.640
in a year or two, so I'm not gonna do it.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.550
So, my point is, launching
ambitious new things,

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.370
which is what Google does, is easy.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:22.320
Supporting those ambitious new things

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.080
for a long time is hard.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.630
And this new announcement
of seven years of software

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:30.870
updates for the Pixel is
the most ambitious software

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.300
support plan we've ever seen
in the smartphone world.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.460
And that's what makes it so awesome

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.140
and so hard to believe at the same time.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.060
Like if I was at Google
and honestly watching this,

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.520
I would really want to make
this promise come true.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.900
I think it's a great promise.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.290
Clearly, I hope more companies
see Google's announcement

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.510
and try to follow and do the same thing

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.760
because making people's
devices more useful

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.240
and more secure for a longer
time is a win for everyone,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.090
for the users, for security,
for the environment

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.470
if people use their phones longer.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.420
So, like if this pans
out, the phones people

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:04.170
are buying today will
be up to date till 2030,

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:07.080
which is an eternity in the
tech world, that is so sick.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:08.340
So, I wanna give them credit

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.640
for an awesome promise,
but it's a promise.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.030
And Google breaks promises all the time.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.980
That's where we're at right now.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:20.640
I want it to be real, but, but we'll see.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.630
Will the Pixel even be
around in seven years?

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:23.820
We don't know.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.490
The Nexus program only lasted five years

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.762
before they killed that and
then started Pixel stuff.

00:06:28.762 --> 00:06:32.490
And we are on year eight of the Pixel now.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:33.712
Will it last seven more years?

00:06:33.712 --> 00:06:34.770
I don't even know.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:37.020
But then actually, this
also ties into another bit

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.090
of a mini trend we've observed lately

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.750
that I wanted to talk about,
which is new announcements

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:47.610
from tech companies that have
features that are coming soon.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:48.510
And this isn't just Google,

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:49.620
this has happened across the board,

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.770
but this has happened where
a device will come out,

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:55.680
but then the new features
aren't available at launch.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.950
So, reviewers aren't even
able to test the new features

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:00.600
until well after the thing is in the wild.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.280
You know, Google just
did this with the Pixel.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.830
The Video Boost feature
they talked about on stage,

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.019
that they said is coming later.

00:07:07.019 --> 00:07:09.810
Video Night Sight is also
not available at launch.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:11.010
That's coming later.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:12.330
But like I said, not just Google.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.610
Apple's done this, actually many times

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.230
with iPhone camera features,

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:19.410
and they've done it again this
year with a bunch of stuff.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.780
The Journal app on the
iPhone is still not there,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.160
coming later this year.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.950
The airdrop transfer
via the internet feature

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:27.390
is also not in the phones yet.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.940
That's still coming later this year.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:31.830
And the double tap feature
for the Apple Watch,

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.390
which is one of the biggest new features

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.970
of the entire watch,
was announced on stage,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.970
but it's still in beta a
month later, and coming soon.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.410
I've just noticed a bunch of these

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:41.400
over the past couple months.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.170
It kind of feels like
I tweeted about this.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:48.170
It kind of feels like the
gaming industry precedent

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.070
where a lot of gaming studios will release

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.860
like a half finished game kind
of to just get it out there

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:53.850
and maybe test the waters,

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.040
and if it hooks, then
maybe they'll throw a bunch

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.810
of patches at it and updates at it.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.330
But releasing half finished
games is just not great.

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:01.980
So, I definitely don't love this trend

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:05.850
for tech and for smartphones and hardware,

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:07.620
but for slightly different reasons.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.800
One, just because it makes
them much harder to review

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.380
if the new feature's not
out and we can't test it

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:14.790
and then we review what's out,

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:18.660
but then the big new
feature comes later, tough.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.030
But I guess I'll just stick
with the thing that I've said

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:26.030
from the beginning, which is
never buy a new piece of tech

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.370
based on the promise of
future software updates.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:30.203
Just don't do it.

00:08:30.203 --> 00:08:31.830
Just buy it for what it is today.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:33.510
And if there's stuff coming
later and it actually

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:35.520
gets delivered on the
promise, some are better

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:38.520
at delivering that than
others, then it's a bonus.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:40.470
It just, it's an extra value add

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:43.350
for the thing that you
are already happy with,

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:45.270
but never buy it just based on the promise

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:46.140
of future updates.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.060
So, that's it, I just had some thoughts

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.680
I wanted to get out into the world.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.740
Hopefully, Google and Apple
and others are listening.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:56.220
Hopefully, they're able to
deliver on these promises,

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.140
but also, hopefully,
they're able to deliver

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:01.658
on things that they promise
at launch when they can.

00:09:01.658 --> 00:09:03.210
And we'll keep an eye on Pixel.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:04.920
I'm testing the phones,
I'll be reviewing 'em.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.050
Subscribe to see the
review when it comes out.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:08.010
That's it for now.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:08.851
Thanks for watching.

00:09:08.851 --> 00:09:10.907
Catch you in the next one, peace.

00:09:10.907 --> 00:09:13.490
(upbeat music)

